41
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:48.029
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): We can see governance in action everywhere we look. And then I didn't have… there's a lot of text, but I didn't put, slides because I thought it would be too busy. In government, legislators create laws to reduce crime among citizens, while law enforcement and the courts make sure those laws are followed.

42
00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:59.289
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In standards organizations, governing bodies published must and shall statements to reduce technical and business risks. Auditors and certification bodies step in to verify that companies are compliant.

43
00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:09.330
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): In sports, rules commissions define how the game is played and what penalties apply, while referees and umpires enforce those rules during the game.

44
00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:28.060
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Schools have their own systems, too. School boards define codes of conduct and academic standards, while principals and teachers apply them day to day to maintain fairness and disciplines. And even at home, parents create family rules to keep children safe and teach responsibility, using chore charts, rewards, responsibilities to hold them accountable.

45
00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:41.739
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Governance, in all these forms, is the process humans use to manage chaos, to create order, trust, and accountability. It's what systems… it's what allows systems, large and small, to function fairly and predictably.

